{:edb/db-before-t 1830 :edb/db-after-t 1831 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535143 50 #inst "2026-10-08T17:57:11.761Z" 13194139535143 true] [17592186105543 1100 "Determine if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/82b99a6e1a271ac0b205a3199b023a582877692c3a61dd4b7144312342fea761.png) is an eigenvector of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/2b9e07d0a0089107dc34bb092167719b6e83a4c41ee9f055949a43549449d65e.png) If so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139535143 true] [17592186105543 1100 "Determine if $1; 1$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ If so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139535143 false] [17592186105543 1145 "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAv &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 4v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v}=4 \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=4$." 13194139535143 false] [17592186105543 1145 "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v}=4 \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=4.$" 13194139535143 true] [17592186105544 1100 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/f35353367a395e7b3560aa6f7ef0da0936cefbced9e76d6d7d8f91426a7a1c9b.png) is an eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/0b4f33107845bf52d80802844bc6faaec6ab9f4bd159a87838497d2f6abe72d1.png) that corresponds to the eigenvalue $2.$\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/defb480233b4e48f90cb07fef771f2fdb62b6d7d5cb116393d74518eb3b37db8.png) is an eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/bb62b77da84018c07d4f3dfefc297684974db78b326bd0736469dc83572ae5b6.png) that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 5,$ then $2\\mathbf{v}$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda = 5.$" 13194139535143 true] [17592186105544 1100 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $2$.\n2. $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0$.\n3. If $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 5$, then $2\\mathbf{v}$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda = 5$." 13194139535143 false] [17592186105544 1145 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n$Av|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}; = 2 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}; = 2v$.\nSince $A\\mathbf{v}=2 \\mathbf{v}$, we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=2$.\n- Statement II is false. An eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector.\n- Statement III is true. We have\n$A(2v) = 2Av = 2(5v) = 5(2v)$.\nSince, $A(2\\mathbf{v})=5(2 \\mathbf{v})$, and $2 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$, this implies that $2 \\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda = 5$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and III only.\"\n\n**Note:** Statement III is true in the general case, too. In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same." 13194139535143 false] [17592186105544 1145 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v}=2 \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. An eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector.\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(2v)=2Av=2(5v)=5(2v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(2\\mathbf{v})=5( 2 \\mathbf{v} ),$ and $2 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ this implies that $2 \\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda = 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and III only.\"\n\n**Note:** Statement III is true in the general case, too. In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same." 13194139535143 true] [17592186105545 1100 "An eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/22ef70dcb0f7d3cba1c9d5514b40800144eb97edd004041ebb3516062a9e3e48.png) corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda=6c$ is  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/d1c2b9c36ec942a1c4113f1ff3a6221028f36c6ac4d109351ebab6bb48f358c4.png) Determine the value of $c.$" 13194139535143 true] [17592186105545 1100 "An eigenvector of $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda=6c$ is $1; 2c$ Determine the value of $c$." 13194139535143 false] [17592186105545 1145 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAv &= λv \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2c\\end{bmatrix} &= 6c\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2c\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 + 2c \\\\ 1 + 4c\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}6c \\\\ 12c^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{2 + 2c = 6c; 1 + 4c = 12c^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2+2c=6c \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting\n\n$$\nc=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + 4c &= 12c^{2} \\\\\n1 + 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} &= 12(\\frac{1}{2})^{2} \\\\\n3 &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solutions of the system is\n\n$$\nc=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n." 13194139535143 false] [17592186105545 1145 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] & =6c[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2+2c \\\\ 1+4c\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6c \\\\ 12{c}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2+2c=6c \\\\ 1+4c=12{c}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2+2c=6c \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $c=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+4c & =12{c}^{2} \\\\ 1+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} & =12{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ 3 & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solutions of the system is $c=\\dfrac{1}{2}$." 13194139535143 true] [17592186105551 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/fdfa0528b5476ed0246679c45fd31824470063a68f33c4c9af0f714ad201538f.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $2.$ Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}.$" 13194139535143 true] [17592186105551 1100 "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $2$. Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1375/Source/Images/204140201746adea135bd375366ced72.png)" 13194139535143 false] [17592186105551 1145 "The eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/bf65510b8cef0e0162e3054bcf31405ac4e4268a759f6493a06c9f0314c1d482.png) corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av=2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/78e1e3c015adad2c826a64e255bd982941dc9fe50679acc2bae3f00b31bd9f39.png)" 13194139535143 true] [17592186105551 1145 "The eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2$. So,\n$Av = 2v$.\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1375/Source/Images/ecd22d908f7242bad08169a51db3796d.png)" 13194139535143 false] [17592186105692 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333544 13194139535143 true] [17592186105692 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333579 13194139535143 true] [17592186105692 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333628 13194139535143 true] [17592186105692 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333635 13194139535143 true] [17592186105693 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333572 13194139535143 true] [17592186105693 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333600 13194139535143 true] [17592186105693 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333607 13194139535143 true] [17592186105693 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333621 13194139535143 true] [17592186105694 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333551 13194139535143 true] [17592186105694 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333565 13194139535143 true] [17592186105694 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333586 13194139535143 true] [17592186105694 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333593 13194139535143 true] [17592186105694 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333642 13194139535143 true] [17592186105711 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333558 13194139535143 true] [17592186105711 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333614 13194139535143 true] [17592186105711 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333649 13194139535143 true] [17592186105711 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333656 13194139535143 true] [17592186105711 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333663 13194139535143 true] [17592186106225 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535143 true] [17592186106225 1100 "Determine if $v = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139535143 false] [17592186106225 1100 "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$  is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139535143 true] [17592186106225 1145 "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=5v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5.$" 13194139535143 true] [17592186106227 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535143 true] [17592186106227 1100 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ then $3v$ is also an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =-7.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$" 13194139535143 true] [17592186106227 1100 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $v = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $λ = 1$.\n2. If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ =-7$ then $3v$ is also an eigenvector corresponding to $λ =-7$.\n3. $v = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0$." 13194139535143 false] [17592186106227 1145 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(3v)=3Av=3(-7v)=-7(3v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(3v)=-7(3v),$ and $3v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-7.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\n- Statement III is false. An eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535143 true] [17592186333544 1017 #edb/ref 17592186333545 13194139535143 true] [17592186333544 1089 "q-106744" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333544 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535143 true] [17592186333544 1100 "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333544 1113 #uuid "b22af89b-1092-506b-925c-dc472e0f7d9f" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333544 1145 "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=\\lambda [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1=\\lambda  \\\\ -2=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333546 13194139535143 true] [17592186333545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333547 13194139535143 true] [17592186333545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333548 13194139535143 true] [17592186333545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333549 13194139535143 true] [17592186333545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333550 13194139535143 true] [17592186333545 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535143 true] [17592186333545 1041 "selection" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333545 1104 #edb/ref 17592186333546 13194139535143 true] [17592186333545 1110 #uuid "34016eca-7fa9-5357-b97a-23860f2ff5c6" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333546 1034 "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333546 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535143 true] [17592186333546 1111 #uuid "c2eb3398-ae19-5ca2-ab18-d8935136c5e0" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333547 1034 "1" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333547 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535143 true] [17592186333547 1111 #uuid "3a0b06b3-08a2-5110-ad50-78ebb74c0b61" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333548 1034 "4" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333548 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535143 true] [17592186333548 1111 #uuid "ae9a45ec-a2d4-5173-9908-784eaabd280e" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333549 1034 "-1" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333549 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535143 true] [17592186333549 1111 #uuid "a60a30d1-8ebf-5d01-9942-d439f4339c66" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333550 1034 "-2" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333550 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535143 true] [17592186333550 1111 #uuid "57a94b9a-0482-5936-b6e8-ad04da8947d1" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333551 1017 #edb/ref 17592186333552 13194139535143 true] [17592186333551 1089 "q-106828" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333551 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535143 true] [17592186333551 1100 "The eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ -k & -1\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =k-1$ is  $[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k.$" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333551 1113 #uuid "ea75fe73-8a8e-588a-8eaf-18860230f59a" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333551 1145 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ -k & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =(k-1)[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-16 \\\\ -4k+4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4k-4 \\\\ -4k+4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-16=4k-4, \\\\ -4k+4=-4k+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-12=4k\\,⟹\\,k=-3.\n$$\n\nThe second equation is true for any value of $k.$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=-3.$" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333553 13194139535143 true] [17592186333552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333554 13194139535143 true] [17592186333552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333555 13194139535143 true] [17592186333552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333556 13194139535143 true] [17592186333552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333557 13194139535143 true] [17592186333552 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535143 true] [17592186333552 1041 "selection" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333552 1104 #edb/ref 17592186333556 13194139535143 true] [17592186333552 1110 #uuid "e6080b57-b10d-5dd9-9f73-e2d70d0903f3" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333553 1034 "3" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333553 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535143 true] [17592186333553 1111 #uuid "83c8a6a6-00b3-5209-9404-fe4e49caa4e8" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333554 1034 "16" 13194139535143 true] [17592186333554 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 131941